s":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in godot 4.4.stable-mono\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.4.stable.mono - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 3 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.7216) - AMD Ryzen 5 5600X 6-Core Processor (12 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nThere seems to be a bug in the latest Godot version with the combination of a CPUParticles2D node  and the physics_interpolation.\n\nLet's assume the following structure: (Project is attached)\nCharacterBody2D\n-> CPUParticles2D \n\nWhen moving the CharacterBody2D, we would assume that the CPUParticles2D would move with it, which it does without physics_interpolation.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/645be0ce-7707-4013-b733-f69b6b90ea7a\n\nBut as soon as we enable physics_interpolation, the CPUParticles2D will just stay at the origin and not move with the CharacterBody2D\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/21821fab-6e50-4882-8867-4f303b378420\n\nWe can mitigate it by enabling the emitting property of the CPUParticles2D, which makes it move again, but is not always an option.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0e1e13d9-d02f-4b12-8386-aebc37d1380c\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Run the attached project\n- Use A/D to move the Characterbody2D\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nThe behaviour can be reproduced with the following project:\n[bug-particle.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19166687/bug-particle.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.